===Noun===
[[Image:Male Knee by David Shankbone.jpg|thumb|A human knee]]
{{en-noun|s|kneen|pl2qual=obsolete or dialectal}}

# {{senseid|en|Q37425}}{{lb|en|anatomy}} In [[humans]], the [[joint]] or the region of the joint in the middle part of the [[leg]] between the [[thigh]] and the [[shank]].
#: {{ux|en|Penny was wearing a miniskirt, so she skinned her exposed '''knees''' when she fell.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=II|canto=V|page=249|passage=He made him ſtoup perforce vnto his '''knee''', / And doe vnwilling worſhip to the Saint, / That on his ſhield depainted he did ſee{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-av
|en
|date=March 21, 1988
|author=w:Vaughn Armstrong
|title=w:Heart of Glory
|series={{w|Star Trek: The Next Generation}}
|publisher=w:Paramount Domestic Television
|genre=[[science fiction|Science Fiction]]
|oclc=26969098
|passage=KORRIS: I have tasted your heart. You have been with them, but you are still "of" us. Do not deny the challenge of your destiny. Get off your '''knees''' and soar. Open your eyes and let the dream take flight.}}
# In the horse and allied animals, the [[carpal joint]], corresponding to the wrist in humans.
# In birds:
## {{lb|en|non-technical}} The [[tarsal joint]], uniting the [[tarsometatarsus]] and the [[tibiotarsus]] ([[homologous]] to the human [[ankle]]).
## The joint uniting the tibiotarsus and the [[femur]], typically hidden beneath [[plumage]].
# The part of a [[garment]] that covers the knee.
# {{lb|en|shipbuilding}} A piece of timber or metal formed with an angle somewhat in the shape of the human knee when bent.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1980|author=Richard W. Unger|title=The Ship in the Medieval Economy 600-1600|page=41
|passage=Deck beams were supported by hanging '''knees''', triangular pieces of wood typically found underneath the timbers they are designed to support, but in this case found above them.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} An act of [[kneel|kneeling]], especially to show respect or courtesy.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Timon of Athens|III|iii|line=36|text=Give them title, '''knee''', and approbation.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2009|author=C. E. Murphy|title=The Pretender's Crown|page=127
|passage={{...}} and he made a '''knee''' to the Caesar of Patna, giving that man all honour due to him.}}
# Any knee-shaped item or sharp angle in a line; an [[inflection point]].
#: {{ux|en|the '''knee''' of a graph}}
# A [[blow]] made with the knee; a [[kneeing]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=Clive Mullis|title=Scooters Yard|passage=Tante was groggy but not quite out so Winnie gave him a '''knee''' to the jaw that Rose had shown her, and that was enough. He slumped like a rag-doll to the floor.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} The presence of a [[parent]] etc., where a young child acquires early knowledge.
#: {{cot|en|lap}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1978|journal=Time|volume=111|issue=18-26|page=49
|passage=The duty is, or should be, a thing taught at one's father's '''knee''', and the structure of the family gently enforces it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Brian Douglas|title=The Eucharistic Theology of Edward Bouverie Pusey|page=113
|passage=This has significant implications for sacramental theology which it seems Pusey even realised in the way he spoke of his early life and of learning all he knew about the Eucharist and the Catholic faith at his mother's '''knee''', {{...}}}}

